# p_d8b3 — Lunchbox and vitamins travel; journal stays (fork of p_a3f1)

Two residents run a conventional two-adult household. Marco leaves for his afternoon-to-night shift around 13:40 and returns around 23:00 on weekdays; on weekends he is home except for a midday errand run (11:30–14:00). Yuki is home most of the day, taking a morning walk (07:00–08:30 weekdays, 10:00–11:30 weekends) and a shorter afternoon errand (14:30–16:00 weekdays only). The dog is fed by Yuki at 07:00 and 18:00, walked at 08:00 and 16:45. Meals are at 07:30, 12:30, and 18:30; evening TV runs 19:30–22:00. Marco bakes on weekday mornings (06:30–08:30); Yuki bakes on weekend afternoons (14:00–16:30). Marco journals and reads at the nightstand 22:30–00:00. Yuki plays guitar 15:00–16:30 on weekdays. Marco does yoga 06:00–06:45 weekdays.

What changed from the parent: the parent claimed nothing of Marco's personal items travels out of the house. The evidence now shows the lunchbox is absent from the cupboard during work hours (10 empty looks) and the vitamins are absent from the counter (5 empty looks). Both are work-related items: the lunchbox is his midday meal at the office, and the vitamins are taken with his work lunch. The journal, however, is a home-based hobby item (he journals in the evening at the nightstand, 22:30–00:00) and is NOT packed for work. The 3 weak-for and 1 against on the journal's out-of-house claim in p_b7c2 are weaker evidence than the lunchbox (10 weak-for) or vitamins (5 weak-for), and the journal's resting spot (nightstand) is in the bedroom, a place he would not naturally grab when heading out the front door at 13:40.

What sets this document apart: between 14:00 and 23:00 on a weekday, lunchbox_marco AND vitamins_marco are OUT_OF_HOUSE, but journal_marco remains at nightstand_b1. If the robot finds the journal out of the house during that window, this document is refuted on that point. If it finds the vitamins at counter_k1 during that window, this document is refuted on the vitamins point.

